Example 1: A participant begins employment on July 13 and works a total of 58 hours between July 13 and July 31. Since the agreement is in effect for less than a full month, the ESW will need to determine the average number of hours worked per week to enter in the ESP module as well as the number of full weeks worked during the month. The calculation will be as follows:

31 (last day of the month in which the assignment began) – 13 (the day of the month when the assignment started) 18 + 1 = 19 days employed during the month

19 days ÷ 7 =2.71 weeks employed during the month

58 hours ÷ 2.71 weeks = 21.4 average hours per week; round down to 21

Example 2: A participant is employed for a full month. The employer reports that the participant worked for a total of 90 hours during the month. The average number of hours worked per week during the month was 21 (90 hours ÷ 4.33 = 20.78 average hours per week; round up to 21).

